1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Welcome back. Every Cloud Code project needs a place for the settings a model shouldn't be

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
free to reinterpret, and that place is settings.json. It gives you a handful of things to

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
configure, permissions, hooks, environment values, and model choice. We'll walk through each one

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
and the mistake people most often make. So what actually goes in this file? Three things matter

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
most. First, permissions, the rules that allow, deny, or ask before Cloud runs a given tool,

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
so risky actions are gated instead of just trusted. Second, hooks, deterministic scripts

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
registered right here that fire at fixed lifecycle events running outside the model to enforce,

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
log, or automate. And third, environment, meaning the variables and model choice you set through

9
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
config so the same values apply every session without being hard-coded into your app.

10
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Permissions, hooks, environment. Hold those three and the rest of the file makes sense.

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Notice these are settings, not suggestions. The model can't talk its way past a deny rule.

12
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Let's start with permissions because they're the layer you'll touch first. A permission rule

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
matches a tool call and lands on one of three decisions. Allow lets a matching call run

14
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
automatically with no prompt because you've already decided that action is safe. Deny blocks

15
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:32,000
a matching call outright so a dangerous or unwanted action never executes at all. And ask pauses and

16
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
prompts you to approve or reject the call, keeping a human in the loop for the sensitive stuff in

17
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:46,000
between. Allow, deny, ask. Most setups lean on allow for routine tools and deny for the handful

18
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:53,000
of things you never want to happen with ask covering the gray area. Now hooks. A hook wires

19
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:59,000
a deterministic script to a specific moment in the agent's lifecycle and the key thing is where

20
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:07,000
you declare it. You put it in settings.json, not in pros. Each hook names the event that should

21
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,000
trigger it and points to the command that runs when that event happens. That's really the whole

22
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:18,000
shape of it and because the hook lives in config rather than in the model's reasoning, it fires the

23
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:26,000
same way every single time no matter what Claude decides to do in the moment. That determinism

24
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,000
is the entire point. When you need something to happen reliably, a hook gives you a guarantee

25
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:39,000
that an instruction never can. So when can a hook fire? Hooks can fire at several lifecycle points

26
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:46,000
and here are five worth knowing, each a natural place to enforce or log. Pre-tool use fires before

27
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,000
a tool call runs, which is the moment you can still stop something. Post-tool use fires after

28
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:58,000
a tool call finishes, once the result is in. Notification fires when Claude surfaces a message

29
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:06,000
to you. Stop fires when a response completes and session start fires when a new session begins,

30
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,000
handy for setup work. These five are among the most common hook points. You don't have to use

31
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,000
all of them. You just pick the moment where the action you care about naturally lives.

32
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Two of those events deserve a closer look because people mix them up

33
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
and it matters. Pre-tool use fires before a pending tool call executes.

34
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:34,000
That timing is everything because from there a hook can deny or ask on a dangerous action

35
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:40,000
and actually stop it before anything happens. That's exactly where guardrails belong. Post-tool

36
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:46,000
use is different. It fires after the call has already finished, so it's great for logging or

37
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,000
reacting to a result, but it can't block something that has already run. So if your goal is to prevent

38
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:59,000
a risky action, reach for pre-tool use. If your goal is to record or respond after the fact,

39
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:06,000
post-tool use is the right tool. When a pre-tool use hook does weigh in on a tool call, it sets a

40
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
field called permission decision and that field has four possible values. Allow approves the

41
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:19,000
pending call and lets it run. Deny blocks it so it never executes. Ask hands the decision to you

42
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:25,000
to approve or reject. And the fourth value is defer, which falls back to your normal permission

43
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:34,000
rules instead of forcing an outcome right there. Allow, deny, ask, defer. Each value maps directly

44
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,000
to how the pending call is handled, so the hook's return decides the outcome deterministically.

45
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:45,000
If a hook returns an empty object, the call is simply left unchanged, which behaves like allow.

46
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Permissions and hooks get the attention, but settings.json also handles the plumbing you'd

47
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:57,000
otherwise hard code. You can set environment variables that your tools and hooks read at

48
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:05,000
runtime, so a value like an endpoint or a flag lives in config, not scattered through your code.

49
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:10,000
You can name a default model for the session and a fallback to use if the first one isn't available.

50
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:17,000
And you can set things like the effort level and other harness options. The theme here is the same

51
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,000
across all of it. Set the value once in one place and every session in that scope picks it up

52
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,000
instead of you repeating yourself or hard coding it. Before we wrap, let's settle the question

53
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:35,000
people ask most. Where does a given change belong? A couple of control layers get confused here.

54
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Settings.json holds machine-readable config, your permissions, hooks, and environment values.

55
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Clod.md holds natural language instructions that Clod reads as context guidance rather than

56
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:57,000
machinery. So the rule is simple. Permissions and hooks go in settings.json, never in prose

57
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:03,000
because prose is a suggestion and config is enforced. One practical note on reloading.

58
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:09,000
Most settings.json keys like permissions, hooks, and environment take effect live,

59
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:14,000
but a couple, model, and output style need a restart or a slash command to pick up.

60
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Let's recap. Settings.json is the machine-readable control layer. Permissions, hooks, environment,

61
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:28,000
and model choice all in one file. It matters because these are enforced controls,

62
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:34,000
not suggestions the model can interpret. Just remember, permissions and hooks live here,

63
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,000
while natural language guidance lives in Clod.md.

64
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:41,000
That covers settings.json permissions and hooks. I'll see you in the next one.

